// HarnessPostTriggerRotateSecret webhook triggers only (409 for cron). Issues a new secret (shown once) and moves the old value to the previous slot, where it keeps verifying deliveries until previous_secret_expires_at. Default grace is 5 minutes when grace_period_seconds is omitted, so deliveries already in flight (signed with the old value) still succeed while the customer updates their external system. After that instant the old value is refused with 401. Pass grace_period_seconds=0 to retire the old secret on this call (breach response), or a positive value (up to the server max, default 1 hour) for a custom window; above the max is 400.
//
// Parameters:
//
//	triggerId: The trigger UUID.
//	gracePeriodSeconds: How long the outgoing secret keeps verifying deliveries.
//	Omit for the default (5 minutes). 0 retires the old secret immediately
//	(compromised-secret path). Positive values set a custom window up to the
//	server max (default 1 hour); above the max is 400. Send at most once with an
//	explicit non-negative integer - bare keys, empty values, non-integers,
//	negatives, and duplicates are 400.
func (sdk *HarnessRuntimeNamespace) HarnessPostTriggerRotateSecret(ctx context.Context, params HarnessPostTriggerRotateSecretParams, opts ...RequestOption) (*RotateSecretResponse, error) {
	return HarnessPostTriggerRotateSecret(ctx, sdk.config, params, opts...)
}

// HarnessPostTriggerWebhook receives a signed webhook delivery from an external system for a trigger. The caller is the external system, and each delivery is authenticated with the trigger's provider-specific signature or token. Successful deliveries are accepted for asynchronous processing; paused triggers and deduplicated retries also receive an accepted response.
//
// The one public, un-team-authenticated endpoint. There is NO DO bearer token: the caller is the customer's external system. Authentication is the per-trigger HMAC secret, verified on every delivery; edge rate-limiting is mandatory. The request body is the provider's RAW body, forwarded verbatim (the HMAC is computed over the exact bytes received); Content-Type is provider-defined and passed through. The signature header depends on the trigger's provider: - github: `X-Hub-Signature-256: sha256=<hex>` (HMAC-SHA256 of the raw body) - gitlab: `X-Gitlab-Token: <token>` (plaintext token equality; GitLab does not sign) - custom: `X-DigitalOcean-Signature: t=<unix>,v1=<hex>` (HMAC-SHA256 of `{unix}.{raw body}`; `do-signature` is also accepted as a transitional alias) On success (including a provider retry that dedupes to an existing firing) it returns 202 with the execution_id. A delivery to a paused trigger returns 202 with no body and queues nothing (deliberately not 409, so providers do not auto-disable the webhook after repeated non-2xx).
//
// Parameters:
//
//	triggerId: The trigger UUID.
func (sdk *HarnessRuntimeNamespace) HarnessPostTriggerWebhook(ctx context.Context, params HarnessPostTriggerWebhookParams, opts ...RequestOption) (*WebhookAcceptedResponse, error) {
	return HarnessPostTriggerWebhook(ctx, sdk.config, params, opts...)
}

